alex: Quick check — where should I be looking for spans emitted by the async-prefetcher worker? I see the root span in Jaeger but almost no child spans for the KV/cache work. Any recent sampling or SDK changes?

sam: For that worker we usually check Jaeger (https://grafana.redwood.local/explore/jaeger) and logs in Logflare. Are you running the Node worker or the Go one?

alex: Node. Using @opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node 1.14.0. Worker is bullmq-based. Suspect context loss across job callbacks.

maria: If it's bullmq, make sure you're initializing the tracer inside the worker process and using AsyncLocalStorage propagation. We saw similar drop when init happened before the worker forked. Also check sampler — infra moved some services to ParentBased(TraceIdRatio) recently.

jordan: Can you paste your tracer init snippet? Also post a tiny failing trace id if possible.

alex: snippet below — I tried to keep it minimal: ```js
const { NodeTracerProvider } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node');
const { registerInstrumentations } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation');
const { JaegerExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-jaeger');
const provider = new NodeTracerProvider();
provider.addSpanProcessor(new SimpleSpanProcessor(new JaegerExporter({serviceName:'prefetcher'})));
provider.register();
registerInstrumentations({instrumentations: []});
```
I init this in my main module that spawns the worker.

maria: there's the problem — if the worker is forked after init, AsyncLocalStorage context won't be hooked. Move `provider.register()` into the worker entrypoint (or re-init after fork). Also try setting propagation explicitly: `const { AsyncHooksContextManager } = require('@opentelemetry/context-async-hooks'); provider.register({contextManager: new AsyncHooksContextManager()});`

sam: +1. Also check sampling: if root sampling rate is low you won't see child spans. For quick debugging switch to `AlwaysOnSampler` then revert. Example: ```js
const { ParentBasedSampler, AlwaysOnSampler } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-base');
provider.register({ sampler: new ParentBasedSampler({ root: new AlwaysOnSampler() }) });
```

alex: switched to AlwaysOn locally — I now see child spans for a few jobs. Looks like sampling was dropping a lot. Which services flipped the sampling config?

maria: infra changed global sampling defaults last week to 10% for non-prod to reduce volume. Prod stayed at 100%. Did you run the worker in the non-prod cluster by chance?

alex: Oh, yes — I deployed the test worker to the staging autoscale group. That explains it. Still need to fix the context hook for forks.

jordan: For a checklist: 1) init tracer in worker process (post-fork), 2) ensure AsyncHooks context manager, 3) verify propagators (`W3CTraceContextPropagator`), 4) temporarily use AlwaysOn for repro, 5) check exporter queue/backpressure (SimpleSpanProcessor vs BatchSpanProcessor).

infra-bot: NOTICE: alert pref-prefetcher-span-drop firing > 5% (rule: pref.fetcher.span_drop_rate) — runbook: https://confluence.redwood.local/runbooks/prefetcher-spans

sre-ops: Saw the alert too. If you can reproduce with AlwaysOn and still see missing child spans, file an incident and tag us. Otherwise we can tune the alert threshold for staging.

alex: Will move init into worker and open a small PR with the AsyncHooks change + a note about sampler toggles. I'll also attach a couple trace ids and a tiny repro job. Ping me for review @maria or @sam. :thumbsup:

maria: Thanks — one more tip: if you use `BatchSpanProcessor`, check that export isn't getting dropped on shutdown — we had a bug where jobs exit before export flush. Add `provider.forceFlush()` in graceful shutdown.

alex: Good call, I'll add that. Last message timestamp here for tracking: 1923460450. Thanks all.
